A||||2||fiero56||'86 2.8 in an '88(TerryK?)||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||09-21-2000||11:03 PM||||I am going to temporarily replace the 2.8 in my '88 Formula with a 2.8 from an '86 Fiero. I don't have the engine yet, but am wondering what will hold me up on installation. First off, '88 cradle design is different than '86. I am unsure if I should use the '88 starter, or '86. Second is alternator. Is it different only in the mounting bracket, or will an '86 alternator work with an '86 engine, in an '88? Also, want to make sure the '86 will work with my '88 ECM. Will the wiring harness plug in to the engine with no problems? Voltages, everything else proper?||||reg||5
Z||000001||fierodave||09-22-2000||03:24 AM||||The 86 engine will work just fine, but some changes need to be made. <BR>From the 88 engine you will need to change the following parts to the 86 engine: Oil pressure sensor, Alternator, alternator heat shield attached to exhaust manifold, exhaust crossover pipe, EGR valve pipe, water pump plug, water crossover pipe, front engine mount bracket., and a/c lower adjustment bracket.  You MUST keep the 86 flywheel/flexplate on the 86 engine. You will not use the 86 engine shock mount bracket. <BR>The 86 engine wiring harness will plug into the 88 computer and C-500 connection. No need to change it or the injector wiring harness. <BR>Many people do not know that in addition to frame, suspension, and brakes, the 88 V6 had a lot of differences in the engine compartment.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.theglobe.com/fierodave/gifs/fierosig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||09-22-2000||11:00 AM||||More stuff than I expected. I was assuming oil pan, alternator, starter, a/c bracket, and engine mount.  I figured the exhaust crossover would work(this is the "y" pipe, right?) Flywheel isn't a problem, the owner of the engine found a flywheel.<BR>At least my cheap temporary engine isn't going to be too costly.<P>Thanks FieroDave. I will print this out and save it for engine installation. ||||reg||1
